  "raw_output": "{\n  \"exercis_type\": \"CEFR English Placement Test\",\n  \"section_id\": 5,\n  \"section_title\": \"Reading Comprehension\",\n  \"cefr_range\": \"B2-C1\",\n  \"reading_text\": \"\\\\\nIn the late 19th century, Paris was a city of dreams and possibilities. The French capital had become an artistic hub with bohemian attitudes that allowed creatives to explore new ideas without fear of censorship or social stigma. Artists such as Pablo Picasso, Vincent van Gogh, and Henri Matisse flocked to the city in search of inspiration and acclaim. The bohemian lifestyle was characterized by a rejection of traditional norms and an embrace of freedom, artistry, and creativity.\\\\\nThe movement known as \\\\\\\"French Impressionism\\\\\\\" flourished during this period. Its members sought to capture the fleeting effects of light and color in their paintings, creating a style that was both vibrant and emotionally charged. One artist who played a significant role in this movement was Claude Monet, whose iconic works like \\\\\\\"Water Lilies\\\\\\\" exemplify the Impressionist approach to painting.\\\\\nHowever, not everyone embraced the new wave of artistry. Critics and conservative society members were skeptical or outright hostile towards this departure from traditional artistic norms. Despite facing numerous challenges, the bohemian spirit persisted in Paris, inspiring generations of creatives to push the boundaries of what was considered \\\\\\\"art\\\\\\\". As a result, the city remains a cultural powerhouse to this day, celebrating its rich history and ongoing vitality in the arts.\\\\\"\",\n  \"questions\": [\n    {\n      \"question_number\": 36,\n      \"question\": \"What was a significant factor that attracted many artists to Paris in the late 19th century?\",\n      \"options\": [\n        \"A) High-quality education system\",\n        \"B) Bohemian lifestyle and freedom of expression\",\n        \"C) Extensive art museums\",\n        \"D) Low living costs\"\n      ],\n      \"correct_answer\": \"B\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"question_number\": 37,\n      \"question\": \"Which artist is known for his iconic painting \\\\\\\"Water Lilies\\\\\\\"?\",\n      \"options\": [\n        \"A) Pablo Picasso\",\n        \"B) Vincent van Gogh\",\n        \"C) Claude Monet\",\n        \"D) Henri Matisse\"\n      ],\n      \"correct_answer\": \"C\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"question_number\": 38,\n      \"question\": \"How did the Impressionist movement differ from traditional artistic norms?\",\n      \"options\": [\n        \"A) Focusing on realistic depictions of everyday life\",\n        \"B) Emphasizing the importance of historical events\",\n        \"C) Capturing fleeting light and color effects in paintings\",\n        \"D) Inspiring social change through art\"\n      ],\n      \"correct_answer\": \"C\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"question_number\": 39,\n      \"question\": \"What was the reaction of conservative society to the new wave of artistry?\",\n      \"options\": [\n        \"A) Open-minded acceptance\",\n        \"B) Hostility and skepticism\",\n        \"C) Support for artistic freedom\",\n        \"D) Indifference\"\n      ],\n      \"correct_answer\": \"B\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"question_number\": 40,\n      \"question\": \"Which of the following statements is true about Paris in the late 19th century?\",\n      \"options\": [\n        \"A) It was a stagnant city with little cultural significance\",\n        \"B) The bohemian spirit persisted despite criticism and challenges\",\n        \"C) Artists were discouraged from exploring new ideas\",\n        \"D) There was a strong emphasis on traditional artistic styles\"\n      ],\n      \"correct_answer\": \"B\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
